1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:05,014
Last time, the Sun vanished for one single second, and Earth barely noticed.

2
00:00:05,014 --> 00:00:06,977
Today, there is no coming back.

3
00:00:06,977 --> 00:00:09,574
The Sun disappears, and it never returns.

4
00:00:09,574 --> 00:00:10,144
No light.

5
00:00:10,144 --> 00:00:10,651
No heat.

6
00:00:10,651 --> 00:00:12,615
No gravity holding us in place.

7
00:00:12,615 --> 00:00:14,072
How long would we last?

8
00:00:14,072 --> 00:00:14,388
Days?

9
00:00:14,388 --> 00:00:14,769
Years?

10
00:00:14,769 --> 00:00:17,936
The answer splits into two very different stories.

11
00:00:17,936 --> 00:00:20,786
One on the surface, and one deep underground.

12
00:00:21,486 --> 00:00:22,745
Let's start the clock.

13
00:00:23,445 --> 00:00:24,058
Time zero.

14
00:00:24,058 --> 00:00:28,905
And for the first eight minutes and twenty seconds, absolutely nothing changes.

15
00:00:28,905 --> 00:00:36,083
The sunlight reaching your eyes left the Sun eight minutes ago, and changes in gravity travel at the very same speed.

16
00:00:36,083 --> 00:00:40,746
So the light already in flight is the last sunlight Earth will ever receive.

17
00:00:41,446 --> 00:00:43,169
Then the final photons arrive.

18
00:00:43,169 --> 00:00:44,433
And the lights go out.

19
00:00:45,133 --> 00:00:48,920
On the day side, the blue sky drains to black almost instantly.

20
00:00:48,920 --> 00:00:51,866
A blue sky is just sunlight scattered by the air.

21
00:00:51,866 --> 00:00:54,632
With no sunlight, there is nothing to scatter.

22
00:00:54,632 --> 00:00:56,255
The stars come out at noon.

23
00:00:56,255 --> 00:00:57,518
Now look at the Moon.

24
00:00:57,518 --> 00:01:00,704
It has no light of its own, it only reflects the Sun.

25
00:01:00,704 --> 00:01:03,410
About a second later, the Moon goes dark too.

26
00:01:03,410 --> 00:01:07,618
A black circle you can only find because it hides the stars behind it.

27
00:01:08,318 --> 00:01:10,571
At the same moment, gravity lets go.

28
00:01:10,571 --> 00:01:17,017
Earth was racing along at almost thirty kilometers per second, its path constantly bent around the Sun.

29
00:01:17,017 --> 00:01:21,335
With nothing left to bend it, Earth does the simplest thing possible.

30
00:01:21,335 --> 00:01:22,399
It goes straight.

31
00:01:22,399 --> 00:01:26,217
In a straight line, at thirty kilometers per second, forever.

32
00:01:26,917 --> 00:01:34,684
After one year of coasting, Earth would be about nine hundred and fifty million kilometers from the spot where the Sun used to be.

33
00:01:34,684 --> 00:01:37,134
That is farther out than Jupiter's orbit.

34
00:01:37,134 --> 00:01:37,910
And the Moon?

35
00:01:37,910 --> 00:01:38,926
It comes with us.

36
00:01:38,926 --> 00:01:45,200
The Moon is held by Earth's gravity, not the Sun's, so the two keep traveling together, a tiny dark pair.

37
00:01:45,899 --> 00:01:48,418
Every other planet does the same thing.

38
00:01:48,418 --> 00:01:50,356
Mercury, Venus, Mars, Jupiter.

39
00:01:50,356 --> 00:01:54,812
Each one flies off along its own straight line, in its own direction.

40
00:01:54,812 --> 00:01:57,460
The solar system doesn't collapse inward.

41
00:01:57,460 --> 00:02:02,497
It comes apart, like a spinning carnival ride whose ropes are all cut at once.

42
00:02:03,197 --> 00:02:06,119
Then the planets begin to vanish from our sky.

43
00:02:06,119 --> 00:02:11,329
Venus, the brightest point of light at night, only shines with reflected sunlight.

44
00:02:11,329 --> 00:02:12,219
It fades away.

45
00:02:12,219 --> 00:02:18,382
Then Mars, then Jupiter, then Saturn, as their last reflected light finishes the trip back to us.

46
00:02:18,382 --> 00:02:23,592
Within about three hours, every planet is gone, and only the distant stars remain.

47
00:02:24,291 --> 00:02:25,975
Strangely, days don't end.

48
00:02:25,975 --> 00:02:31,608
Earth keeps spinning once every twenty four hours, because nothing is there to stop it.

49
00:02:31,608 --> 00:02:33,875
But a day no longer means anything.

50
00:02:33,875 --> 00:02:35,299
No sunrise, no sunset.

51
00:02:35,299 --> 00:02:40,867
Just the same stars wheeling overhead, again and again, in a sky that is always night.

52
00:02:41,567 --> 00:02:44,447
Here is an effect almost nobody thinks about.

53
00:02:44,447 --> 00:02:45,535
The tides change.

54
00:02:45,535 --> 00:02:49,695
The Moon raises most of our ocean tides, but the Sun helps a lot.

55
00:02:49,695 --> 00:02:55,391
According to NOAA, the Sun's tide-raising force is about forty six percent of the Moon's.

56
00:02:55,391 --> 00:03:02,047
Without it, tides get weaker, and the extra high spring tides around full moon and new moon simply stop.

57
00:03:02,747 --> 00:03:04,026
Now the cold begins.

58
00:03:04,026 --> 00:03:06,519
Our air holds surprisingly little heat.

59
00:03:06,519 --> 00:03:13,359
Caltech planetary scientist David Stevenson notes that the atmosphere's warmth only matters for a few days.

60
00:03:13,359 --> 00:03:21,414
After that, the ground and the oceans carry the load, and they leak heat into space every second, with nothing coming back in.

61
00:03:22,114 --> 00:03:23,053
One week in.

62
00:03:23,753 --> 00:03:28,785
Today, the average temperature at Earth's surface is about fifteen degrees Celsius.

63
00:03:28,785 --> 00:03:32,726
Within roughly a week of darkness, it falls below minus eighteen.

64
00:03:32,726 --> 00:03:34,667
That is zero degrees Fahrenheit.

65
00:03:34,667 --> 00:03:39,456
Colder than your home freezer, everywhere on the planet, including the tropics.

66
00:03:39,456 --> 00:03:42,185
The surface of the sea begins to freeze over.

67
00:03:42,885 --> 00:03:44,534
Life feels it immediately.

68
00:03:44,534 --> 00:03:51,703
Photosynthesis, the process that feeds almost every food chain on the planet, stops the moment the light is gone.

69
00:03:51,703 --> 00:03:54,303
Most plants would die within a few weeks.

70
00:03:54,303 --> 00:03:56,524
Strangely, the giants last longest.

71
00:03:56,524 --> 00:04:02,106
Large trees store so much sugar that they could hang on for decades, frozen and dormant.

72
00:04:02,806 --> 00:04:03,617
One year.

73
00:04:04,317 --> 00:04:09,259
After a year, Stevenson estimates the surface would be below two hundred kelvin.

74
00:04:09,259 --> 00:04:14,325
That's about minus seventy three degrees Celsius, or minus one hundred Fahrenheit.

75
00:04:14,325 --> 00:04:20,936
The cold of a deep Antarctic winter night, but everywhere, all year round, with no summer ever coming back.

76
00:04:21,636 --> 00:04:22,961
But here is the twist.

77
00:04:22,961 --> 00:04:24,769
The oceans don't freeze solid.

78
00:04:24,769 --> 00:04:26,637
Not for a very, very long time.

79
00:04:26,637 --> 00:04:29,048
Ice floats, and ice is a good insulator.

80
00:04:29,048 --> 00:04:33,990
As the frozen lid gets thicker, it works like a blanket over the water underneath.

81
00:04:33,990 --> 00:04:40,679
And down on the sea floor, hydrothermal vents keep pumping out hot, mineral rich water, heated by Earth itself.

82
00:04:40,679 --> 00:04:44,717
The microbes living there feed on chemical energy, not on sunlight.

83
00:04:44,717 --> 00:04:47,790
Some of them could keep going for a very long time.

84
00:04:48,490 --> 00:04:50,866
So where does that deep heat come from?

85
00:04:50,866 --> 00:04:52,754
Earth has a furnace of its own.

86
00:04:52,754 --> 00:04:58,176
Heat left over from its formation, plus radioactive elements slowly decaying in the rock.

87
00:04:58,176 --> 00:05:01,952
It leaks out through the crust at about forty seven terawatts.

88
00:05:01,952 --> 00:05:05,120
Sounds enormous, until you compare it with sunlight.

89
00:05:05,120 --> 00:05:10,663
The sunlight hitting Earth delivers about one hundred and seventy three thousand terawatts.

90
00:05:10,663 --> 00:05:14,867
Earth's own heat is roughly one part in three thousand seven hundred.

91
00:05:14,867 --> 00:05:18,521
Without the Sun, that tiny red square is everything we have.

92
00:05:19,222 --> 00:05:21,035
Now jump ahead millions of years.

93
00:05:21,735 --> 00:05:27,480
The surface finally settles where that faint trickle of inner heat balances what escapes to space.

94
00:05:27,480 --> 00:05:29,767
Stevenson puts it around thirty kelvin.

95
00:05:29,767 --> 00:05:33,109
That's about minus two hundred and forty degrees Celsius.

96
00:05:33,109 --> 00:05:35,571
Long before that, the air itself gives up.

97
00:05:35,571 --> 00:05:40,144
Nitrogen freezes at minus two hundred and ten degrees, oxygen a little colder.

98
00:05:40,144 --> 00:05:46,652
The atmosphere falls to the ground as snow, burying the planet under a frozen layer of what used to be its sky.

99
00:05:47,352 --> 00:05:48,034
And people?

100
00:05:48,034 --> 00:05:51,756
On the surface, survival would be measured in years at most.

101
00:05:51,756 --> 00:05:53,616
But we are a stubborn species.

102
00:05:53,616 --> 00:05:58,206
Iceland already heats almost nine out of ten homes with geothermal energy.

103
00:05:58,206 --> 00:06:00,501
Nuclear reactors don't need sunlight.

104
00:06:00,501 --> 00:06:09,122
Stevenson suggests that a small number of people could survive for a very long time by moving miles underground, where the rock stays warm.

105
00:06:09,122 --> 00:06:12,843
Life would look less like a city, and more like a submarine.

106
00:06:12,843 --> 00:06:17,804
Food would come from greenhouses lit by lamps, powered by the planet's own heat.

107
00:06:17,804 --> 00:06:20,968
Every calorie would have to be earned the hard way.

108
00:06:21,668 --> 00:06:25,260
And far away, the Sun would still be shining for a while.

109
00:06:25,260 --> 00:06:27,528
Its last light keeps racing outward.

110
00:06:27,528 --> 00:06:30,427
Neptune sees a normal Sun for four more hours.

111
00:06:30,427 --> 00:06:31,940
Voyager one, a full day.

112
00:06:31,940 --> 00:06:37,044
Anyone watching from Proxima Centauri would see our Sun for more than four years.

113
00:06:37,044 --> 00:06:40,826
From the Andromeda galaxy, for two and a half million years.

114
00:06:40,826 --> 00:06:44,985
A star that no longer exists, still glowing in someone else's sky.

115
00:06:45,685 --> 00:06:48,532
Meanwhile, Earth keeps moving through the galaxy.

116
00:06:48,532 --> 00:06:56,842
It still carries the Sun's old speed around the Milky Way, so it would still complete a lap roughly every two hundred and thirty million years.

117
00:06:56,842 --> 00:06:57,481
Just alone.

118
00:06:57,481 --> 00:06:59,340
And it wouldn't even be unusual.

119
00:06:59,340 --> 00:07:04,860
NASA researchers estimate our galaxy may hold about twenty times more rogue planets than stars.

120
00:07:04,860 --> 00:07:07,592
Worlds drifting in the dark with no sun at all.

121
00:07:07,592 --> 00:07:10,090
Earth would simply become one more of them.

122
00:07:10,790 --> 00:07:12,746
Could we ever reach another star?

123
00:07:12,746 --> 00:07:16,361
Earth's speed is fixed at about thirty kilometers per second.

124
00:07:16,361 --> 00:07:23,770
At that pace, crossing even the four point two light years to Proxima Centauri would take more than forty two thousand years.

125
00:07:23,770 --> 00:07:29,222
And space is so empty that Earth would almost certainly never pass close to any star at all.

126
00:07:29,922 --> 00:07:31,775
So, let's put it all together.

127
00:07:31,775 --> 00:07:35,729
Not instant darkness, but eight more minutes of an ordinary day.

128
00:07:35,729 --> 00:07:39,188
Earth doesn't fall into anything, it just goes straight.

129
00:07:39,188 --> 00:07:41,659
And life doesn't end in a single moment.

130
00:07:41,659 --> 00:07:42,400
It retreats.

131
00:07:42,400 --> 00:07:46,477
From the surface, to the ocean floor, to the warm rock deep below.

132
00:07:47,177 --> 00:07:48,920
We think of the Sun as a lamp.

133
00:07:48,920 --> 00:07:52,696
It's really an anchor, holding a whole family of worlds together.

134
00:07:52,696 --> 00:07:56,414
Take it away, and everything we know drifts apart into the dark.

135
00:07:57,114 --> 00:07:59,238
Next time, we zoom all the way out.

136
00:07:59,238 --> 00:08:01,180
How big is the universe, really?

137
00:08:01,180 --> 00:08:05,671
The answer is bigger, and stranger, than the number you've probably heard.

138
00:08:05,671 --> 00:08:08,523
Subscribe to Cosmic Scale so you don't miss it.

139
00:08:08,523 --> 00:08:09,616
See you out there.
